        title: "Decision Logic: The Hidden Architecture That Governs Execution",
        content:
            "An established company makes thousands of small decisions every week: which lead gets called first, whether an order ships to a customer with an aging balance, when a job overrun needs a change order, and who can release a purchase above a threshold. Those decisions add up to the company's operating reality, and the logic behind most of them has never been written down.\n## Implicit logic and its limits\nIn a young company, decisions follow leadership's judgment directly. Questions route to the people who started the business, and the logic lives in their heads. As the company grows, routing every question upward creates a bottleneck, so authority gets delegated.\nDelegation without written logic produces variance. Each manager develops a personal rule set, and the same situation handled by two people produces two outcomes. Standards exist on paper while practice drifts.\n## Five parts of a decision\nEvery repeatable decision has parts that can be designed. A trigger defines when the decision is required, such as a job crossing its estimated hours or a balance passing 60 days. Criteria define how options are weighed, and authority defines who may decide at each level of consequence. An escalation path defines where a case goes when the standard logic does not fit, and documentation records the logic so it can be reviewed, taught, and improved.\n## Where the logic lives today\nIn most companies the logic is scattered across policy documents few people read, software settings few people understand, and the experience of long-tenured staff who apply it without stating it. Logic in those places conflicts, leaves with the people who hold it, and cannot be audited without reconstructing who decided what and why.\n## Decision logic is what AI executes\nAn agent that routes a lead applies scoring logic, and an agent that flags a margin overrun applies threshold logic. When the company has not defined that logic, someone invents it during implementation, often a developer guessing at what leadership would want. The agent then decides on assumptions, and trust erodes.\nCompanies with written decision logic deploy AI on rules they have already validated, and outcomes become predictable.\n## Governance in practice\nGovernance at the operating level is the system by which decisions get made and accountability gets assigned. Written decision logic is how strategy reaches execution without depending on leadership's presence in every conversation.\n## Approval levels in Quanton OS\nQuanton OS makes decision logic part of the system. Work runs inside the rules and approvals leadership sets, configured per function during the engagement and recorded in the Engagement Agreement.\nDeterministic, low-risk internal work such as scheduled reports, data categorization, and ledger postings the company's accounting rules already map runs with full autonomy. Draft content, scheduled follow-ups, routine supplier communication, and messages from approved templates run on silent approval: the agent prepares and queues the action, and it proceeds after a review window unless someone steps in. Free-form customer communications, quotes, invoices, contracts, changes to what the company charges, financial decisions, compliance filings, and hiring decisions are a hard block, and nothing happens until a person explicitly approves.\nSome controls are fixed for every client. Any action on an obligation, such as a filing, a renewal, or a contract notice, always waits for explicit approval. Approval levels are enforced at the point of action, agents cannot change their own governance settings, and after go-live the levels change only by a signed change order.\n\nWritten decision logic turns leadership's judgment into something the company can run on. Every hour spent defining it pays back across every decision that follows.",
