        id: 10,
        title: "AI Accuracy Is a Structure Problem",
        content:
            "Ask small businesses that already use AI what holds them back, and the most common answer is accuracy. In the Federal Reserve Banks' 2025 Small Business Credit Survey, 46% of AI users named accuracy as a top challenge, ahead of every other concern.\nWaiting for better models is the instinctive response. Large studies of AI deployment point somewhere else.\n## What the failure data says\nMIT's Project NANDA published The GenAI Divide: State of AI in Business 2025 after interviewing 52 executives, surveying 153 leaders, and reviewing 300 public AI deployments. It found that about 95% of generative AI pilots delivered no measurable impact on profit and loss, and it attributed the gap to brittle workflows, weak contextual learning, and misalignment with day-to-day operations instead of the quality of the underlying models.\nGartner reached a parallel conclusion about AI agents in June 2025. It predicts that more than 40% of agentic AI projects will be canceled by the end of 2027 because of escalating costs, unclear business value, or inadequate risk controls, and it warns that integrating agents into legacy systems often disrupts workflows and requires costly modifications.\nBoth findings point at the environment AI is placed into, not at the model.\n## The context lives outside the record\nAccounting platforms do their job well, with sales, bank activity, and expenses typically together in one ledger. What sits elsewhere is the operating context around each transaction: the estimate behind a job, the schedule it runs on, the inventory it consumes, the change orders approved along the way, and the approvals that released each purchase. An estimating tool, a scheduling app, a shared spreadsheet, and a project manager's notes each hold a piece.\nAn AI assistant answers from whatever it can reach. Connected to the books, it sees an invoice and a payment, and it cannot see that the job behind them absorbed two unbilled change orders and a week of overtime. Its answer is accurate about the ledger and wrong about the business.\n## The rules were never written\nEvery company runs on decisions with rules behind them. Can an order ship to a customer whose balance is 60 days past due? Who signs off on a change order above a set threshold? When does a purchase order need a second approval? In many companies the answer depends on who picks up the phone, and the rule exists only as habit.\nAI cannot follow a rule nobody wrote down. Given a vague instruction, it improvises, and improvisation on credit, pricing, and commitments is precisely what leadership cannot accept.\n## A model is producing the figures\nLarge language models generate likely text. Asked for a margin, a forecast, or a reorder quantity, they return a figure that reads correctly and may not be correct, and a calculation that is right most of the time is unacceptable for a quote, a payroll run, or a cash forecast.\nFigures need deterministic computation: code that returns the same answer from the same inputs every time, with every input traceable to the record. AI belongs on the other side of that line, drafting the explanation of what the numbers mean.\n## How Quanton OS closes all three\nQuanton OS consolidates accounting, payroll, inventory, operations, and customer records into one system of record the company owns. Agents read and write directly to it, so they operate on the actual structure of the business instead of guessing at it through a third-party connection.\nWork runs inside the rules and approvals leadership sets. Before any output reaches its approval level, it is checked against the company's business rules: brand, what the company charges, promised dates and quantities against capacity and stock, contract terms, financial documents, people and safety, and outbound communication standards.\nShared engines compute and check everything. Code computes every number. AI drafts and explains and never produces a figure.\n\nAccuracy improves when AI works from a complete record, written rules, and computed figures. A better model on a fragmented company only produces more convincing errors.\nSources: Federal Reserve Banks, 2026 Report on Employer Firms: Findings from the 2025 Small Business Credit Survey (March 2026). MIT Project NANDA, The GenAI Divide: State of AI in Business 2025 (July 2025). Gartner, Gartner Predicts Over 40% of Agentic AI Projects Will Be Canceled by End of 2027 (press release, June 25, 2025).",
