        title: "Six Million Ownership Transfers and What a Buyer Will Underwrite",
        content:
            "About six million U.S. small and medium-size businesses will face ownership changes by 2035 as baby boomer owners retire, according to the McKinsey Institute for Economic Mobility. In the Exit Planning Institute's 2023 National State of Owner Readiness Report, 75% of business owners said they would like to exit within the next ten years.\nEach transition will run through buyers, lenders, or successors who ask a version of one question: does the company keep performing once the people who built it step back?\n## What diligence tests\nA buyer or lender is underwriting the next several years, not purchasing last year's results. Diligence probes whether reported earnings trace to transactions, whether customer and supplier commitments are documented and kept, whether compliance is current, and whether the knowledge that runs daily operations lives anywhere other than in a few people.\nEvery gap becomes a point of negotiation: unverified earnings get adjusted, commitments that exist only in memory get priced into the deal or pushed into an earn-out, and dependence on key people turns into retention requirements for the very people a transition was meant to free.\n## Where company knowledge lives today\nIn many established companies, the operating playbook is distributed. Pricing logic sits with an estimator, supplier terms with a purchasing lead, special handling for key customers with an account manager, and filing dates and license renewals in someone's calendar. Day to day, the company runs well because those people are good at their jobs.\nA transition tests that arrangement directly, by asking how much of the company's value depends on each person staying.\n## Building a record a buyer can trust\nA structural remedy takes time, which is why it matters years ahead of any transaction. Every transaction, document, and obligation needs to live in one governed record. Every commitment the business owes or is owed, from contract terms and filings to insurance and licenses, needs an act-by date, escalating reminders, and a named responsible person, and every action, exception, and approval needs an audit trail that cannot be altered after the fact.\nQuanton OS builds that record, and the company owns the operational core it runs on outright. Across the company, everyone works from one version of the truth. Commitments are kept, compliance stays current, and knowledge stays when people leave. The company's value and borrowing position are ready for a lender or buyer at any moment.\n## Value that compounds\nOver time, a company that ran on the memory of a few people comes to run on a system that improves every month it operates, and becomes easier to scale, finance, and sell.\n\nSix million transfers will separate companies that can prove how they run from companies that can only describe it.\nSources: McKinsey Institute for Economic Mobility, The Great Ownership Transfer: A New Era of Business Stewardship (February 2026). Exit Planning Institute, 2023 National State of Owner Readiness Report.",
