        id: 14,
        title: "Where Leadership's Week Actually Goes",
        content:
            "Michael Porter and Nitin Nohria spent more than a decade tracking how chief executives use their time. Their Harvard Business Review study, published in 2018, followed 27 CEOs of large companies and coded more than 60,000 hours of their work. On average the CEOs worked 62.5 hours a week and attended 37 meetings, and meetings consumed 72% of their total work time.\nLarge-company CEOs work with chiefs of staff, executive assistants, finance departments, and layers of management built to absorb coordination before it reaches the top. Established companies with a few dozen to a few hundred employees have far less of that buffer. Coordination that a large company spreads across a management layer falls directly on the President and a small leadership team.\n## One calendar, three kinds of work\nOperating work moves transactions through the company: orders, receipts, invoices, payroll, field work. Managing work keeps those transactions coordinated, checked, and on time, while leading work sets direction on capital, people, pricing strategy, and where the company competes next.\nCalendars never label which kind of work a meeting holds. A production meeting, a customer call, or a cash review looks like leadership on the schedule, and much of the time inside it goes to managing: finding out what is true, chasing who owns the next step, and making small calls that a defined rule could have made.\n## A worked week\nConsider a composite week at a 60-person specialty contractor run by a President, a controller, and an operations manager. It is an illustrative company, and the pattern is common.\nMonday opens with the weekly production meeting. Ninety minutes go to rebuilding job status from three project managers' spreadsheets. Two change orders come up for approval, and the President asks whether the material for one of them has already been ordered. Nobody in the room knows, so the approval waits until someone checks after the meeting.\nOn Tuesday a long-standing customer calls the President directly about a date that slipped. From there the President calls the superintendent, then purchasing, then the customer again. Three people spend most of an hour reconstructing a delay the schedule already showed to anyone who looked at it.\nWednesday brings the cash review with the controller. Receivables have aged on two accounts, and a supplier wants tighter terms. Half the meeting goes to assembling the numbers and checking them against the job files, and the decision about the supplier relationship slides to next week.\nThursday is bid review for a new project. Margin assumptions in the estimate come from last year's completed jobs, because current job costs will not be reliable until the month closes. Leadership prices a significant commitment on numbers it already suspects are stale.\nFriday collects the rest: questions about overtime on the payroll, a license renewal discovered in someone's inbox with nine days left, and a missing safety form on an active site.\nSorted honestly, most of the week went to managing. Leading got the fragments between: the pricing strategy behind the bid, the supplier decision, and a hiring conversation that moved to next week for the third time.\n## Why delegation alone does not fix it\nDelegation is the usual answer, and it moves the load instead of removing it. Hand the production meeting to the operations manager and the operations manager now spends Monday rebuilding status from the same three spreadsheets. Hire a coordinator to chase change orders and the coordinator becomes the person everyone asks, which makes the coordinator the new single point of failure.\nManaging work stays expensive because of where its inputs live. Status sits in spreadsheets that someone has to reconcile, rules sit in people's heads, and commitments sit in inboxes. Whoever takes on the managing load inherits those conditions, so the hours follow the work to whoever holds it. Fixing the load requires changing the conditions: one current record, written rules for what is allowed and who decides, and a defined path for every exception.\nLarge companies solve part of the problem with headcount, which is why the CEOs in the Porter and Nohria study could spend so much of their week in meetings at all. Established companies cannot match that management layer and should not try. Building it out of salaried coordinators repeats the original problem at a higher payroll.\n## What the system carries\nEvery item in that week's managing load has a structural home in Quanton OS. Job status, change orders, receipts, and costs live in one system of record the company owns, so the production meeting starts from current status instead of rebuilding it. Before a change order reaches its approver, the system has already checked it against material on order, stock, and the schedule.\nStatus updates sit with the Customer Experience department lead and its specialist agents, and a slipped date surfaces as an exception routed to the person who owns it before the customer has to call. Receivables, collections, and billing sit with the Finance department lead, and leadership sees a forward view of cash instead of assembling one. Every commitment the business owes or is owed, from licenses to insurance and filings, is tracked with an act-by date, escalating reminders, and a named responsible person, and any action on an obligation waits for explicit approval. Time and attendance run through the worker mobile app under the People and Team department lead, so payroll questions arrive with the hours already reconciled.\nA Governing Agent coordinates all seven department leads, detects conflicts between departments, and routes every exception to the right person with the analysis already done.\n## What leadership keeps\nLeadership keeps the decisions that need judgment, and the system is built to deliver them in better condition. Quotes, proposals, financial decisions, hiring decisions, and changes to what the company charges are a hard block: nothing happens until a person explicitly approves. The bid gets priced on current job costs, the supplier decision arrives with the cash position attached, and the hiring conversation stops competing with status meetings for the same hours.\nApproval levels are set per function during the engagement, and after go-live they change only by a change order leadership signs. The line between what runs on its own and what waits for the President becomes a decision leadership makes once, not a judgment call repeated every Monday morning.\nMeetings change character as a result. With status already visible, the production meeting becomes a review of exceptions and decisions, and meetings decide instead of reconstructing status.\n## Measuring the change\nA leadership team can see its own pattern in a week. Tag every meeting and call on the calendar as operating, managing, or leading, based on what actually happened in it, not on its title. Status reviews, chasing, and reconciling count as managing; decisions on direction, capital, people, and pricing strategy count as leading. Most teams that run this exercise find the leading column thinner than they expected.\nPhase 1 of every engagement captures baseline measurements, and leadership time lost to managing is one of the five categories its ROI estimate quantifies. Quarterly reviews then compare the leadership calendar after deployment against that baseline, so the shift from managing to leading is documented instead of claimed.\n\nLeadership's week is not short on hours. It is short on hours spent leading, and the gap closes when managing work runs on a system instead of on the calendar.\nSources: Michael E. Porter and Nitin Nohria, How CEOs Manage Time, Harvard Business Review, July to August 2018 (figures confirmed against the MGMA reprint published by permission of Harvard Business Publishing). The contractor week is an illustrative composite, not a client.",
